Having --jobs be per-repository does not make sense when serving a lot
of repositories. Eg, with 10k repositories, even --jobs=2 means there
can be 20k concurrent worker threads. Which is entirely too many to be any
kind of resource limit. At the same time, only 2 jobs per repository is
entirely too few. And using any reasonable number of --jobs, like 100,
with so many repositories leads to high memory use.

If Annex state for repositories changes to be demand loaded and freed with
a LRU cache, as seems likely for
[[todo/p2phttp_startup_time_with_many_repositories]], then this would
change to --jobs times the size of the cache. That is better, but still too
many. With the LRU cache sized to 100 repositories (which seems reasonable
if you have 10k total), and --jobs=100, 10k worker threads is still too
many.

A plan to fix this:

* A single quantity semaphore for --jobs that gates new job state creation.
* One prototype Annex state per active repository, to use with dupState to
  get a new job state.
* A counter for the total number of idle Annex states.
* For each repository, have a list of idle Annex states. Once an Annex
  state has been used to service a request, add it to its repository's
  list. If there are too many idle Annex states, remove one from the
  list of the oldest repository in the LRU cache.

[[!tag projects/INM7]]
